+\begin{observation}[Arbitrary cutting marks need not be locally extendible]
+\label{obs:bl-mark-not-local}
+The requirement $u_i\in\AM^{c_i}_2$ defines a useful
+\emph{locally extendible} marked fat-tree space. For a restricted
+monoid it can exclude legal cutting marks of globally admissible
+variable words, and hence is not yet the full space intended by
+Definition~\ref{def:marked-word-reduction}.
+\end{observation}
+\begin{proof}
+In the binary parity monoid of Proposition~\ref{prop:bl-parity},
+the canonical blocks $v11$ and $v$ are admissible: each has an odd
+number of variable occurrences and an even number of constant $1$'s.
+Their concatenation $v11v$ admits a marked cut after $v1$, giving the
+marked blocks $v1$ and $1v$. The cut lies strictly after the last
+occurrence of the first variable and before the first occurrence of
+the next, so it is permitted by the marked-word definition.
+However the first proposed width-two row would satisfy
+$u(())=()$ and $u(a)=a1$ for $a\in\{0,1\}$.
+Its variable block would therefore be $v1$, whose constant-$1$
+parity is odd. No element of the parity monoid can extend that
+row. Thus the globally permitted marked word has no such
+locally extendible row representation. The obstruction concerns
+extendibility, not the numerical placement of cuts.
+\end{proof}
+This distinction must be retained in future definitions: either
+restrict explicitly to locally extendible marked rows, or use a
+\emph{globally coherent} marked profile (including partial line states)
+which need not admit separate monoid completions at every mark.
+In the latter case the candidate marks become genuine approximation
+data, and completion/fusion must be proved for their joint witnesses.
+
